./node_modules/vue-web-component-wrapper/docs/disable-shadow-dom.md:5:When the `disableShadowDOM` option is set to `true`, the web component will render its content in the light DOM, allowing the styles and scripts to affect the rest of the host application. This can be useful when you want to share styles or scripts between the web component and the host application.
./node_modules/vue-web-component-wrapper/docs/disable-shadow-dom.md:30:By disabling the Shadow DOM, you can have more control over the styling and behavior of your web component, allowing it to interact more seamlessly with the rest of the host application. However, be aware that disabling the Shadow DOM can also expose your web component to potential clashes with the host application's styles and scripts. Use this feature judiciously based on your specific use case and requirements.
./node_modules/vue-web-component-wrapper/docs/index.md:29:Our latest feature allows you to automatically replace `:root` selectors with `:host` in your CSS styles. This is particularly useful when working with CSS frameworks or custom styles that define variables or styles on the `:root` selector. By replacing `:root` with `:host`, these styles are correctly scoped within your web component's Shadow DOM.
